{"type":"data","nodes":[null,{"type":"data","data":[{"mission":1,"hero":561},{"id":2,"agency":3,"agency_full":4,"sector":5,"dest":6,"color":7,"year":8,"status":9,"departure_date":10,"arrival_date":11,"transit_days":12,"round_trip":13,"vehicle":14,"payload":15,"delta_v":16,"data_quality":17,"credit":18,"links":19,"flight_data_quality":27,"fleet_refs":28,"flight":38,"name":533,"type":534,"first":535,"description":536,"events":537,"vehicle_stage":40},"artemis5","NASA","National Aeronautics and Space Administration","gov","MOON","#0B3D91",2028,"PLANNED","2028-11-01","2028-11-05",4,true,"SLS Block 1 (upper stage to be confirmed) + crewed lander (Starship HLS or Blue Moon)","Orion + 4-person crew; second crewed landing, start of lunar base work","~7 km/s (round trip)","partial","© NASA — Artemis V. Planned for late 2028 as the second crewed landing of the Artemis era and the start of work towards a lunar surface base at the south pole. SLS remains the crew launcher through Artemis V; NASA has said the configuration stays as close to Block 1 as possible.",[20,24],{"l":21,"u":22,"t":23},"Artemis V — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artemis_V","intro",{"l":25,"u":26,"t":23},"NASA adds mission to Artemis lunar program, updates architecture (2026-02-27)","https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-adds-mission-to-artemis-lunar-program-updates-architecture/","sparse",[29,32,35],{"id":30,"role":31},"sls-block-1","launcher",{"id":33,"role":34},"orion","spacecraft",{"id":36,"role":37},"lc-39b","launch-site",{"launch":39,"cruise":43,"arrival":46,"totals":56,"events":60,"cislunar_profile":104},{"vehicle_stage":40,"mass_at_tli_kg":41,"source":42},"SLS upper stage (to be confirmed)",26500,"Artemis V (planned, late 2028); upper stage not confirmed by NASA — values carried from the Artemis IV profile",{"tcm_count":44,"source":45},2,"NASA Artemis architecture documents (planned-mission target; outbound TCM + NRHO insertion correction)",{"v_infinity_km_s":47,"periapsis_km":48,"inclination_deg":49,"orbit_insertion_dv_km_s":50,"source":51,"entry_velocity_km_s":52,"edl_system":53,"edl_duration_s":54,"touchdown_velocity_ms":55},0.85,3000,90,0.45,"NASA Artemis IV mission plan; NRHO entry burn ~0.45 km/s vs Apollo's 0.89 km/s direct LLO insertion (NRHO is energetically favourable)",0.15,"powered",100,1,{"total_dv_km_s":57,"tli_or_tmi_dv_km_s":58,"source":59},7.2,3.05,"NASA Artemis architecture (planned); SLS-Orion + HLS combined ledger",[61,66,71,76,80,84,89,94,99],{"met_days":62,"type":63,"label":64,"description":65},0,"launch","Launch — SLS Block 1 / Orion (planned)","Kennedy Space Center LC-39B, planned for late 2028. Artemis V is planned as the second crewed landing of the Artemis era, the start of work towards a base near the lunar south pole. SLS Block 1 launches Orion with four crew; the upper stage is still to be confirmed.",{"met_days":67,"type":68,"label":69,"description":70},0.13,"tli_or_tmi","Trans-lunar injection","The upper-stage burn sends Orion and its crew on a 4-day coast to Near-Rectilinear Halo Orbit, where the commercial lander waits.",{"met_days":72,"type":73,"label":74,"description":75},1.48,"tcm","MCC-1","First mid-course correction.",{"met_days":77,"type":73,"label":78,"description":79},1.5,"MCC-2","Approach-phase refinement.",{"met_days":12,"type":81,"label":82,"description":83,"dv_km_s":50},"loi","NRHO insertion + lander rendezvous","Orion captures into Near-Rectilinear Halo Orbit — an elongated lunar orbit with a 3,000 km perilune and a 70,000 km apolune, much cheaper to reach than Apollo's circular low lunar orbit (~0.45 km/s against Apollo's 0.89 km/s). The crew docks with the waiting lander.",{"met_days":85,"type":86,"label":87,"description":88},5,"descent_start","Lander powered descent — lunar south pole","Two crew descend in the lander while two remain in Orion. Crew, lander and landing site are not yet chosen; the aim is to begin longer stays and the groundwork for a surface base.",{"met_days":90,"type":91,"label":92,"description":93},12,"ascent","Lander ascent + return to NRHO","The lander lifts off after the surface stay and carries the two surface crew back to NRHO, where they transfer to Orion.",{"met_days":95,"type":96,"label":97,"description":98,"dv_km_s":50},14,"tei","Trans-Earth injection","Orion leaves NRHO on its return trajectory, about ten days after arriving.",{"met_days":100,"type":101,"label":102,"description":103},18,"earth_return","Splashdown — Pacific Ocean","Planned for late 2028. Orion re-enters Earth's atmosphere at lunar-return velocity (~11 km/s) and splashes down in the Pacific.",{"source_tier":105,"parking_orbit":106,"tli":109,"translunar":111,"lunar_arrival":113,"return":115,"waypoints_km":117},"tier_1_5_hybrid",{"altitude_km":107,"inclination_deg":108,"revs":77},325,32.5,{"dv_kms":58,"c3_km2_s2":110},-1.7,{"type":112},"hybrid_free_return",{"type":114,"altitude_km":48,"inclination_deg":49,"periselene_km":48},"lor_orbit",{"type":116,"dv_kms":50},"tei_lor",[118,120,122,126,130,134,138,142,146,149,153,157,160,164,168,172,176,180,184,188,192,196,200,203,208,213,218,223,228,232,237,242,247,252,257,262,267,272,277,282,287,292,297,302,307,312,317,322,327,332,337,341,345,349,353,357,361,365,369,373,377,381,385,389,393,397,401,404,408,412,416,420,425,429,433,437,441,445,448,452,456,460,464,468,472,476,480,484,488,492,496,500,504,508,512,516,520,524,528,531],[62,119,62,62],6703,[67,121,62,62],-6703,[123,124,62,125],0.3636,-7123,961,[127,128,62,129],0.5455,-7433,1807,[131,132,62,133],0.7273,-7724,2758,[135,136,62,137],0.9091,-7993,3834,[139,140,62,141],1.0909,-8231,5065,[143,144,62,145],1.2727,-8428,6488,[77,147,62,148],-8591,8610,[150,151,62,152],1.6364,-8626,10118,[154,155,62,156],1.8182,-8569,12482,[44,158,62,159],-8342,15371,[161,162,62,163],2.1818,-7860,18967,[165,166,62,167],2.3636,-6986,23544,[169,170,62,171],2.5455,-5497,29521,[173,174,62,175],2.7273,-3000,37562,[177,178,62,179],2.9091,1204,48765,[181,182,62,183],3.0909,8437,65010,[185,186,62,187],3.2727,